   "run": {
    "status": "answered",
    "final": "To list failed systemd units using `systemctl --failed`, you can run the following command:\n\n```bash\nsystemctl --failed\n```\n\n### What it does:\n- `systemctl` is the systemd utility for managing system services.\n- The `--failed` option specifically lists all **failed** services (those that are not running but are expected to be).\n\n### Example output:\n```bash\n\u25cf bluetooth.service - Bluetooth\n   Loaded: loaded (/lib/systemd/system/bluetooth.service; enabled; vendor preset: enabled)\n   Active: failed (Result: exit-code) since Mon 2023-10-05 12:34:56 UTC; 2h ago\n   Process: 1234 ExecStart=/usr/bin/bluetoothd\n   Status: The package bluetooth.service was active, but is no longer running.\n```\n\n### Notes:\n- This command only shows services that are **failed**, not all services.\n- It does **not** show inactive or stopped services \u2014 only those that failed to start or are in a failed state.\n- If no units are failed, the command will output nothing.\n\n### Optional: Show only the unit names\nIf you just want the names of failed units:\n\n```bash\nsystemctl --failed --no-pager | awk '{print $1}'\n```\n\nOr to get a clean list:\n\n```bash\nsystemctl --failed | awk '{print $1}'\n```\n\nLet me know if you want to filter by a specific service or check the status of a particular unit.",
